  "output": "I've now covered the full codebase (it's a small, early-stage Next.js app — ~30 files, mostly Server Components with a handful of client components). Here's an honest assessment: most of the categories you asked about don't apply yet because the app is too early-stage, but I found several real, actionable issues.\n\n## Summary of what actually exists here\n\nThis is a pre-launch Next.js 16 app (Clerk auth + Drizzle/Postgres). There are no client-side data-fetching loops, no lists of components mapping over live query results with heavy renders, and no `useEffect`/polling yet — so classic \"N+1 in a loop of fetches\" and \"memory leak from uncleared interval\" bugs aren't present *today*. But there are real, concrete issues worth fixing now before they compound.\n\n### 1. Real N+1-shaped issue: `countSourcesBySide()` fetches and re-parses every row just to count them\n`lib/server/sources.ts:23-30`\n```ts\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const all = await listSources();          // SELECT * + zod-parses every row\n  return {\n    crm: all.filter(...).length,\n    warehouse: all.filter(...).length,\n    synthetic: all.filter(...).length,\n  };\n}\n```\n`GetStartedPage` only needs counts, but this pulls full rows (including `config` jsonb) and runs `sourceSchema.parse` on each just to throw the data away. Fine at 10 rows, wasteful once sources scale. Push the aggregation into SQL instead:\n\n```ts\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  await connection();\n  const rows = await getDb()\n    .select({ kind: sources.kind, count: sql<number>`count(*)` })\n    .from(sources)\n    .groupBy(sources.kind);\n  // fold rows into { crm, warehouse, synthetic }\n}\n```\n\n### 2. Duplicate work risk: `listSources()` has no request-level memoization\nRight now it's called once per page, so it's not hurting anything, but nothing stops a future page from calling `listSources()` twice in one render tree (e.g. once in a layout, once in a page) and issuing the query twice. Wrap it in React's `cache()` now, while it's a one-line change:\n\n```ts\nimport { cache } from \"react\";\n\nexport const listSources = cache(async (): Promise<Source[]> => {\n  await connection();\n  const rows = await getDb().select().from(sources).orderBy(sources.createdAt);\n  return rows.map((row) => sourceSchema.parse(row));\n});\n```\nThis dedupes calls within a single request without adding a cross-request cache (which you don't want here, per the \"request-time state\" comment already in the file).\n\n### 3. `entities` table has an index but no obvious query path uses it yet\n`lib/db/schema.ts:115` defines `entities_run_source_external_idx` on `(runId, sourceId, externalId)` — good — but there's no `lib/server/*.ts` reader for `entities`/`matches`/`conflicts` yet. When you build the runs/conflicts pages, watch for the classic N+1 there: fetching a run's matches, then looping to fetch each match's two entities individually. Use a single join or `inArray()` batch fetch instead:\n\n```ts\n// Bad (N+1):\nfor (const match of matches) {\n  const entityA = await db.select().from(entities).where(eq(entities.id, match.entityAId));\n}\n\n// Good:\nconst ids = matches.flatMap(m => [m.entityAId, m.entityBId]);\nconst rows = await db.select().from(entities).where(inArray(entities.id, ids));\n```\n\n### 4. React re-renders: nothing problematic today, but one thing to watch\n`components/console/nav-rail.tsx` is a client component calling `useUser()` and `usePathname()` on every route change — fine, it's cheap and scoped to the rail. No `useState`/derived-computation patterns exist elsewhere that would cause avoidable re-renders. `SourceTable` maps over server-fetched props with stable keys (`source.id`) — correct.\n\n### 5. Caching: `getDb()` in `lib/db/index.ts` already does the right thing\nIt lazily connects and caches the client on `globalThis` to survive HMR — no leak risk there, no changes needed.\n\n### 6. No memory leaks found\nNo `setInterval`/`addEventListener`/subscriptions without cleanup in any client component — the `motion` usage is all mount-time animation with no manual listeners.\n\n### 7. No redundant computations found in render paths\nThe only per-item work in `SourceTable` (`SOURCE_KIND_LABEL[source.kind]` lookups) is O(1) and unavoidable.\n\n**Bottom line:** items #1 and #2 are the only concrete, do-it-now fixes. Item #3 is a heads-up for when you build the runs/conflicts/matches pages — that's where a real N+1 is likely to get introduced first, given the schema shape. Everything else you asked about (re-renders, leaks, redundant computation) doesn't have a live instance in this codebase yet.\n",
